Chapter: RealApproxEquality
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 `~` and `~0` are the only equality Real has, because `==` on a Real is
 refused outright (CDX2085). Both lower to their own emitters, and neither
 took a type until 2026-07-28: they mapped an IEEE pattern to a monotonic
 ordinal with `sar 63`, which broadcasts the sign from the register's TOP
 bit. An f32 is zero-extended into the low half, so its sign sits at bit 31
 and was never read -- every single-precision value looked positive.

 Measured before the fix: the first three lines all answered False while
 their f64 twins answered True. The f64 pair is in this file precisely so
 the failure could not be mistaken for the comparison being wrong in
 general; it is the control that says the defect is about WIDTH.

Section: Probe

 Negative zero and positive zero are the same number. Any comparison that
 says otherwise is reading the sign bit in the wrong place.

  neg-zero : Real approximate
  neg-zero = bits-to-real-approx #80000000

  pos-zero : Real approximate
  pos-zero = bits-to-real-approx #00000000

 One ULP either side of zero, which are two ULP apart and well inside the
 four-ULP tolerance.

  tiny-pos : Real approximate
  tiny-pos = bits-to-real-approx #00000001

  tiny-neg : Real approximate
  tiny-neg = bits-to-real-approx #80000001

 The same question one width up, where the transform is known to be right.

  d-neg-zero : Real
  d-neg-zero = bits-to-real #8000000000000000

  d-pos-zero : Real
  d-pos-zero = bits-to-real #0000000000000000

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
    print-line-uni ("f32 -0 ~0 +0 (want True)  = " & show (neg-zero ~0 pos-zero))
    print-line-uni ("f32 -0 ~  +0 (want True)  = " & show (neg-zero ~ pos-zero))
    print-line-uni ("f32 tiny straddle (True)  = " & show (tiny-neg ~ tiny-pos))
    print-line-uni ("f64 -0 ~0 +0 (want True)  = " & show (d-neg-zero ~0 d-pos-zero))
    print-line-uni ("f64 -0 ~  +0 (want True)  = " & show (d-neg-zero ~ d-pos-zero))
  end
